Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy":"<h4>Introduction</h4><p>In contemporary discussions of meritocracy, two perspectives are common. Either meritocracy is the champion of individual abilities and effort, or it is a sham, a societal misconception which only entrenches existing power and wealth differentials. I argue that this is a false binary: meritocracy can be both, <em>at difference times.</em> This argument is developed within the context of liberal societies—the only context in which meritocracy is seriously advanced as a fairness claim, since it presupposes commitments to individual liberty and family autonomy that liberalism independently affirms.[^1] Through a diachronic analysis, we will find that meritocracy is either illiberal, or fundamentally an incoherent, self-defeating concept. In this paper, I specifically attack desert-based meritocracy, the principle that the allocation of social goods is intrinsically justified because individuals deserve outcomes proportional to their merits. [@mulliganMeritocracy2023, sec. 2.2] I concede that instrumental merit, i.e., it is clear that allowing the most qualified to hold a position is a sound principle, regardless of how one is able to become the most qualified, is valid to a certain degree.[^2] <strong>In short, meritocracy, by necessity, it promotes an unacceptable level of structural inequality, which gives us good normative reasons to reject it as a viable principle of societal organization.</strong></p><h4>Justifying Meritocracy</h4><p>Desert-based meritocracy (DBM) claims to be a <em>just</em> system of allocation where positions and rewards go to those who earn them through relevant talent and effort, disregarding irrelevant characteristics.[@fishkinJusticeEqualOpportunity, p. 22] But what does it mean to be just? We can distinguish between two interpretations.[@fishkinJusticeEqualOpportunity, p. 22; @rawlsTheoryJusticeRevised1999, sec. 47] (1) <em>Procedural justice</em> states that if the procedure involved in the distribution of social goods is justice, then the outcome of that distribution is just. John Rawls’ terms this “pure procedural justice,” giving the analogy of a fair gambling game, where as long as the procedure is carried out according to fair rules, the outcome is just—though it may result in a differential distribution of goods. [@rawlsTheoryJusticeRevised1999, p. 74-76] (2) <em>Background justice</em> regards the circumstances which must hold for procedural justice to be meaningful, that is, that preexisting institutions must be fair before the procedure executes, otherwise the procedure just launders whatever inequalities preceded it. It seems that any strong meaning of “justice” must satisfy the criteria from both interpretations of the term. Disregard the former, and there is no guarantee that the the people who are the hardest working are compensated for their efforts. Disregard the latter, and the society merely entrenches extant inequities over time. Therefore, to be a just system of distribution, meritocracy must satisfy justice in this strong sense. Let’s consider (2) in more specificity. Following the convention first explicated in Rawls’s <em>A Theory of Justice</em>, we can distinguish between two why in which background justice may be violated: natural contingencies and social contingencies.[@rawlsTheoryJusticeRevised1999, sec. 12] Natural contingencies represent differences in ability which are innate, broadly encompassing traits such as strength, intelligence, and creativity. Social contingencies represent differences in ability which are due to social circumstances outside one’s control, such as the one’s family wealth, class background, and level/quality of schooling. Background justice for DBM therefore requires equalization of social contingencies specifically; call this Equality of Life Chances (ELC), following Fishkin.[@fishkinJusticeEqualOpportunity, p. 32-34] Social contingencies have no bearing on one’s merits, and simply launder pre-existing inequality while talent and effort are themselves natural endowments, and to require their equalization would dissolve M entirely, since there would be nothing left to reward. In fact, this is not merely a stipulation: it reflects the meritocrat's own preferred account, shared by Mulligan and Miller alike, that natural talent is the legitimate desert base and social circumstances are the distorting factor.[@millerTwoCheersMeritocracy1996; @mulliganJusticeMeritocraticState2017, sec. 4.3] In short, we can define what I term the <em>necessary condition</em>; that is, M is normatively justified if and only if ELC holds. On this note, however, the meritocrat may argue that the argument proves too much. Alan Zaitchik argues that, since it seems like a pre-theoretical certainty that some people deserve things, any argument whose conclusion is “nobody deserves anything” must have a false premise.[@zaitchikDeservingDeserve1977] But this objection misidentifies the target. The biconditional is an institutional claim, not a claim about personal desert. It claims that any desert-based justification for meritocracy fails at the societal and institutional level, because the system is self-defeating. In fact, this distinction is well-established in the literature: Feinberg distinguishes between institutional entitlements and genuine personal desert,[@feinbergDoingDeservingEssays1970, p. 55-94] and McLeod separates “p-desert,” or a pre-institutional moral claim grounded in a person's character, effort, or conduct, from “i-desert,” or what a person deserves relative to the rules of an existing institution.[@pojmanWhatWeDeserve1999, p. 186-95] We can granting Zaitchik that individuals have genuine personal desert claims while leaving the institutional biconditional entirely untouched. So, the necessary condition stands.</p><h4>The Necessary Condition</h4><p>Liberalism generally upholds the principle of Family Autonomy (FA): that parents the right to invest time, attention, culture, values, and parenting in one’s children.[@fishkinJusticeEqualOpportunity, p.39-40] Some have objected to the entire concept of FA. Harry Brighouse and Adam Swift have argued that FA is not a unitary principle but decomposes into (a) “familial relationship goods,” realized through intimate partialities like bedtime stories, conversation, moral modeling, and (b) transferable positional advantages like elite schooling and bequest, which are not constitutive of family value. Only (a) generates genuine liberal duties of non-interference; (b) can be regulated, taxed, or abolished without damaging the family as such.[@brighouseFamilyValuesEthics2014] In fact, I accept this decomposition by defining FA rather narrowly, with factors such as the wealth and connections of a family left out. Factors such as a family’s wealth and power instead falls under the purview of ELC. Still, there is research which suggests that even these immaterial things as described in (a) has real effect on the distribution of wealth; in other words, that ELC and FA are difficult to separate. Specifically, the type of family a child is born into has wide-ranging impacts on the future success of the child. For instance, studies have shown how family types,[^3] class, racial backgrounds, and even parenting styles significantly affect student outcomes.[@fisherComparativeStudyUnderachieving;  @potterPsychosocialWellbeingChildren2017; @vangelderenQualityLifeAdolescents2012; @lareauUnequalChildhoodsClass2003; @segallIfYoureLuck2011; @gheausWhatAbolishingFamily2018] So, it seems that Brighouse-Swift's decomposition fails empirically. If this is the case, then, given the the necessary condition and FA, we can deduce that M is incompatible with liberal societies. The meritocrat might raise two objections here:</p><ol><li>The meritocrat accepts the empirical evidence, but argues that it is evidence of a fixable policy, not a structural incompatibility.</li><li>The ELC does not require perfect equalization, only that life chances are not predictable from morally arbitrary facts beyond some acceptable threshold.[@lopezEsTrilemaFishkin1996] </li></ol><h4>The Diachronic Argument</h4><p>To address these concerns, I will develop an argument that demonstrates the conceptual incompatibility of FA and ELC, which I term the <em>diachronic argument</em>. Assuming a liberal society and granting that all persons are equalized at some initial time ($ELC_{t_{0}}$) and that at $t_{0}$ FA is irrelevant ($\\lnot FA_{t_{0}}$; e.g., all families have the exact same structure and parent in the exact same manner), we can imagine the following to occur:</p><ol><li>$M$ operates from $t_{0} \\rightarrow t_{1}$ under perfectly fair conditions.</li><li>$M$ produces winners (due to people’s natural contingencies), who are then free to exercise $FA$ at $t_{1}$.</li><li>$FA$ means that parents are free to investment differentially in children, which means $\\lnot ELC_{t_{2}}$.</li><li>Operating $M$ once again, and since the winners of $M$ continue to differentially invest in their progeny, furthering $\\lnot ELC$, $\\neg\\,{ELC}<em>{t_2} \\rightarrow \\neg\\,{ELC}</em>{t_3} \\rightarrow \\cdots \\rightarrow {ELC}_{t_n}$.</li><li>Therefore, even granting ${ELC}<em>{t_0}$, ${FA}</em>{t_1} \\rightarrow \\neg\\,{ELC}_{t_n}$ for all $n \\geq 2$.</li><li>Then, since it fails to satisfy the necessary condition, liberal societies must reject $M$.</li></ol><p>Importantly, this argument is different from Rawls’ luck objection. The luck objection states that, <em>at a single moment,</em> your talent is based on arbitrary and thus morally irrelevant factors.[@logarRawlssRejectionPreinstitutional2013] Thus, you cannot be said to deserve the rewards that flow from those talents, and the basic structure should not allow them to determine distributive shares unchecked. The diachronic argument, however, grants that the initial allocation of rewards was fully deserved, and shows instead that M is necessarily self-refuting. The injustice is therefore not located in the arbitrariness of talent at a single moment, but in the very structure of the intersection of M and liberal societies. This means that one can reject the luck objection entirely and yet accept the diachronic critique; in fact, this argument is stronger than the luck objection since it offers more concessions to the meritocrat.</p><h4>Some Considerations</h4><p>Returning to the aforementioned concerns, as long as one accepts the presence of natural contingencies, the diachronic argument ensures that M eventually undermines itself. The meritocracy might then argue for the equalization of natural contingencies in order to resolve the argument, but this faces several problems. First, it is unclear whether this would be liberal in nature; its hard to see how a society could equalize something like IQ or creativity without massive intrusions into the rights of families to their own children. Second, there are some natural contingencies which are extremely difficult, if not impossible, to mitigate. For instance, the location or time at which someone is born. It would be extremely challenging for a society to be able to coordinate the location and time of birth of every individual—yet, one’s birth location is clearly relevant for one’s future prospects, and thus must be mitigated given the overarching goal of mitigating natural contingencies.[@shacharBirthrightLotteryCitizenship2009; @rawlsTheoryJusticeRevised1999, sec. 12] Having examined the specific objections towards the incompatibility between FA and ELC, let us examine some other, a general defenses of meritocracy. In <em>Justice and the Meritocratic State</em>, Thomas Mulligan presents a rather unique defense of meritocracy, arguing from a metaphysical front. Drawing from Kripke’s concept of the essentiality of origin,[@kripkeNamingNecessity1980, p. 111-116] Mulligan argues that since one’s innate traits are metaphysically necessary to your identity, those traits are therefore not “arbitrary” in the morally relevant sense. One could not wish to be born with different talents, since the person with different talents would not be you. Napoletano (2022), however, demonstrates that this argument conflates metaphysical necessity with moral non-arbitrariness: that a trait could not have been otherwise does not entail that it is a fair basis for distributing social rewards.[@napoletanoCanEssentialityOrigin2022] But this refutation is in fact unnecessary for the basic function of the diachronic argument, since it operates through social contingencies. Natural contingencies are only relevant at $t_{0}$, though it then begins to produce social contingencies which become the main problem for the meritocrat. To address one final objection, a meritocrat might argue for the re-imposition of ELC after the application of FA in each time period. But this concedes that M requires permanent external correction to sustain its own justificatory conditions, meaning $M$ is not a self-standing principle of justice but a temporary corrective mechanism dependent on possibly illiberal interventions of state to restore a favorable condition. It is hard to see any meritocrat accepting this as a satisfactory interpretation of meritocracy.</p><h4>Conclusion</h4><p>Framing meritocracy as a binary is a false dichotomy. It fundamentally creates the situation for its own demise, even when it’s given the charitable concessions at to begin with. So, if we want to preserve meritocracy, the right question to ask is is: are we willing to give up the very foundations of the liberalism in order to do so?</p><p>[^1]: For the classic case in favor of liberalism, see [@millLiberty1864]. Furthermore, liberalism has proponents across the political spectrum, ranging from left-egalitarians, e.g. Ronald Dworkin in his [@dworkinTakingRightsSeriously1977] to liberal egalitarians, e.g. Rawl’s [@rawlsTheoryJusticeRevised1999] to right-libertarians, e.g. Nozick’s [@nozickAnarchyStateUtopia1974]. [^2]: Literature such as [@mulliganMeritocracy2023] and [@danielsMeritMeritocracy1978, p. 207-208] distinguish these two forms of meritocracy and how belief in instrumental meritocracy does not necessitate a corresponding belief in desert-based meritocracy. [^3]: Types of family arrangements, i.e., two parent nuclear family, one parent family, same-sex parent families, etc.</p>","A ruler must be able to choose competent subordinates":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the most important traits of a good leader is that they are able to choose good subordinates who are competent and loyal.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Very often, the most successful rulers are the ones who were able to find the best subordinates.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Fundamentally, this principles seems to arise out of the fact that both the time, energy, and scope of a leader’s personal abilities are limited, and therefore, much of the administration of a state must be left up to subordinates, who thus ought to be as competent as possible. A leader who is able to delegate tasks, especially ones that they themselves are not well-versed in, to proficient subordinates expands his administrative capabilities.</p><h5>Legalist School</h5><p>The Legalist School argued that, in fact, the only job of the ruler is to pick competent officials, who then run the state for him. The ruler mere sets the goals, and the officials carry out the directive to the best of their ability. Those who succeed are rewarded, while those who fail are punished.</p><ul><li>The ruler must delegate since what the ruler contributes is the <em>unity of decision</em>, not personal ability. Therefore, the ability to delegate must never by delegated to another individual, and neither should the force to enforce punishment upon wayward officials.</li></ul><p>To the ruler, the methods with which his goals are accomplished is irrelevant—such matter are the purview of the officials that he delegates.</p><h4>References</h4>","An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A conceit sustains one comparison over many scenes rather than exhausting it in a line, which lets a work develop an idea structurally instead of stating it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the device by which a work argues without asserting, and it is what distinguishes a controlling image from ordinary figurative language.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li><em>Hamlet</em> is built on several, including the recurring images of disease and rot in the state, which return in different mouths and different scenes without ever being named as the play’s subject.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together\">Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together</a><ul><li>The general criterion of which the conceit is one technique.</li></ul></li></ul>","By becoming one with the cosmos, one finds the Tao":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Once one is able to become one with the universe, where the boundaries between the individual and the world dissolves, one is essentially rendered immortal and one finds absolute happiness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the final goal of the Taoist, that is, to become the Taoist sage who has become one with the universe.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When one becomes one with the universe, things the trouble the average person in their daily lives fail to disturb the sage any longer, since they themselves have genuinely been molded into a part of the cosmos. Life and death become much less meaningful, since in the grand scheme of things (from the view of the cosmos), they no longer matter.</p><h5>Zen</h5><p>A similar concept is in Zen Buddhism, since achieving enlightenment means being unable to see the distinction between things in the cosmos, thus becoming one with the Tao.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One must discard knowledge\">One must discard knowledge</a><ul><li>This is the means by which one is able to become one with the universe.</li></ul></li></ul>","Confucianism divided over whether human nature is innately good or innately evil":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While both were branches of Confucianism, Mencius argued that all humans were naturally good-natured, and Xunzi argued that all humans were naturally ill-natured.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the ideological diversity that existed in Confucianism following its founding, and The domination of Xunzi’s ideas would shape Confucianism until the Neo-Confucian revival.</p><h4>Mencius</h4><ul><li>He developed the idea of the four cardinal virtues:<ul><li>Benevolence</li><li>Righteousness </li><li>Wisdom</li><li>Propriety </li></ul></li><li>Argued that all humans were innately good.</li></ul><ul><li>Also developed further by Xunzi.<ul><li>Argued that all humans are innately evil.</li><li>Argued for the importance of learning to develop oneself and make oneself better.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Why?</h4><ul><li>Part of reason that Xunzi and Mencius have conflicting ideas is that they have different definitions of human nature: Mencius defines it as the <em>potential</em> for what one could become, while Xunzi defines it as one's <em>natural dispositions</em>.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately evil\">All humans are innately evil</a><ul><li>Xunzi’s thesis.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately good\">All humans are innately good</a><ul><li>Mencius’s thesis.</li></ul></li></ul>","Great powers incessantly strive to become the hegemon":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The end goal of all great powers is to ultimately become the great hegemon of the world. This is something that impossible to be separated from what it means to be a great power.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the defining traits of a great power. That being said, no state has ever succeeded in becoming the hegemon, and no state probably will be able to become the hegemon.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are different explanations for this. For classical realism, this because states are inherently power seeking and want to dominate and conquer other states. For offensive realism, the reason for this is not that states are fundamentally power hungry and seek to dominate other states, but rather that out of their own safety, states must unceasingly acquire more power in order to ensure their own safety.</p><ul><li>This is justified by the three assumptions that underly offensive realism.</li></ul><p>States will never be satisfied not becoming the hegemon since:</p><ol><li>They are never sure how much relative power a state must secure so that it would be safe from its rivals.</li><li>The power dynamic might change in the future, and thus it is best for them to act now.</li></ol><p>These assumptions also force the conclusions that states care about <strong>relative power</strong> and not <strong>absolute power</strong>. This means that states will forgo an increase in their power if it is the case that one of their rivals will acquire relatively more power. It is also the case that states will never be able to become a global hegemon, because it is simply too difficult to achieve world subjugation. Therefore, the ideal goal for a state realistically is to become a regional hegemon.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>For liberals, this is not the case. They believe that in a world of only “good” powers, there would be no further need to acquire more power.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The international system forces states to be aggressive with each other\">The international system forces states to be aggressive with each other</a><ul><li>Here I describe the three asumptions.</li></ul></li></ul>","How characters are framed in a shot can demonstrate the relationship or the mental state of characters":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>How characters are positioned within shots, especially as it relates to how they are framed together with other characters, gives an insight into the relationship between the characters. Additionally, the way they are shot can also give us an indication of the mental state of the character.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is cinematic technique used by films such as <em>The Third Man</em> or <em>Fargo</em>.</p><h4><em>Fargo</em></h4><p> In the film, Margie is always filmed in the same frame as norm, indicating their close relationship. Additionally, when Mike moves closer to her during their conversation, she explicitly asks him to move to the other side of the table, moving them out of the same frame.   Marge is always portrayed in the interview scene with Jerry as seen over his shoulder, showing her connection with others and her connection with the world at large. On the other hand, Jerry is shown in a claustrophobic manner where the camera gets in his face and there is nothing to locate him in a space (it is merely a blank wall behind him).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Dutch Angle can be used to convey a sense of disorientation and confusion with the surrounding world\">The Dutch Angle can be used to convey a sense of disorientation and confusion with the surrounding world</a><ul><li>This is a specific example of this principle in action.</li></ul></li></ul>","Immigrants of all types are a net benefit to the US":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>All types of immigrants contribute to helping reduce the federal debt, because they consume less resources than native citizens, and sometimes contribute more in tax revenue than native citizens.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This challenges the ideas that immigration is bad for the economy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>From 1994 to 2023, immigrants generated $24.2 trillion in tax revenue compared to triggering only $13.6 trillion in costs. When factoring the amount saved in interest, this means that immigrants generated $14.5 trillion in surplus.<ul><li>In fact, without immigrants, the US debt-to-GDP ratio would have surpassed 205% of GDP, which is widely considered to be the threshold beyond which a country enters an economic crisis.</li><li>A lot of government spending is fixed, and does not expand based on the population. This means that immigrants actually help diffuse the costs of such programs among the US population.</li></ul></li><li>The average immigrant generates 23% more taxes than the citizen. This is because<ul><li>Immigrants are more 20% more likely to be prime working age, meaning that the state does not have to pay for their education, nor their retirement benefits (yet, and even then, not all immigrants qualify) and</li><li>Immigrants are 12% more likely to be employed.</li></ul></li><li>The majority of the taxes that immigrants pay are hidden, which is a result of the nature of the US tax system.<ul><li>This manifests through payroll taxes, property taxes (passed through to renters), and corporate incidence (corporate tax generations driven by labor expansion).</li></ul></li><li>In fact, even illegal immigrants contribute to the surplus, since while they have a much lower tax compliance rate, they receive 75% less in old-age benefits and use half the educational resources of the US on average.</li><li>Second generation immigrants are likewise lucrative, since they have a the highest educational rate.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In the US, direct state expenditures are often &#x27;submerged&#x27; through indirect tax breaks\">In the US, direct state expenditures are often 'submerged' through indirect tax breaks</a><ul><li>Likewise, many tax receipts are not properly documented due to the existence of things like payroll taxes.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Compensating differentials partly explain why jobs have different pay\">Compensating differentials partly explain why jobs have different pay</a><ul><li>Some of these immigrants may be more likely to take more dangerous jobs.</li></ul></li></ul>","Matching people to roles by merit may be less efficient than letting them choose":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Allocating positions by measured merit assumes the allocator knows where each person is most productive, whereas allowing people to select their own occupations uses information about preference and local circumstance that no central assessment can capture.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This turns the standard efficiency argument for meritocracy against it, and it does so on the meritocrat’s own consequentialist ground.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Hayek makes the argument in this form, and it is the same objection he raises to central planning generally, since in both cases the planner must aggregate knowledge that exists only in dispersed and tacit form.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Socialism has a knowledge problem\">Socialism has a knowledge problem</a><ul><li>The general form of the objection.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem\">The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem</a><ul><li>The same argument in its original economic setting.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge\">Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge</a><ul><li>Why this objection is fatal only to the consequentialist defence.</li></ul></li></ul>","Merchants were the lowest social class in Imperial China":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Merchants were viewed as the lowest social class in Imperial China since they were viewed as being merely people who exchanged items and did not create anything productive. They did not work as the peasants did.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Perhaps this derogatory view of merchants was what ultimately halted the development of a free market in China compared to Europe.</p><h4>References</h4>","Moby-Dick (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows Ishmael, a young sailor who joins the whaling ship <em>Pequod</em> under the command of Captain Ahab, an obsessive and vengeful man determined to hunt the great white whale Moby Dick, which previously destroyed his ship and severed his leg. Ahab’s pursuit ultimately brings about disaster: Moby Dick destroys the <em>Pequod</em>, kills Ahab and nearly the entire crew, while Ishmael alone survives to tell the story.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the greatest works of American (world) literature, exploring themes of friendship, fate, nature, obsession, and humanity’s desire to understand the unknown.</p><h4>Raw Notes</h4><ul><li>This book is anti-Enlightenment; reality itself does not process clear lines. Human nature and reality cannot fully be rationalized.</li><li>“I promise nothing complete; because any human thing supposed to be complete, must for that very reason infallibly be faulty.”</li><li>Onto-theology: the idea that the universe can be neatly tied together where there is one correct interpretation of the world and one correct categorization.<ul><li>Everything has a reason, place, and a moral purpose.</li></ul></li><li>The danger is when we mistake our maps for reality. This is the fundamental idea of the book.</li><li><strong>Whiteness</strong>: there are two conceptions<ol><li>The sterility of the universe/the underlying logical structure of the universe that is devoid of human meaning. It’s the dead void of science.</li><li>“Blankness that is full of meaning.” By attempting to capture all the meaning there is in the world, we simply create “blankness/whiteness,” which is the most ambiguous of all. This mirrors the very physical nature of light itself.<ul><li>Humans should be able to deal with the radical variety that exists in the world.</li><li>We inherently want to pursue a grand theory of everything.</li><li>The weaver god’s loom is all of the interconnection of human existence and culture. However, if one wants to synthesize all of this, its just chaos (whiteness).</li></ul></li></ol></li><li>The solution is learning to live with the multifaceted world. It means to have distance with the weaver god’s loom. It means to be able to see that the whiteness of the world is, in fact, the combination of all the colors of the rainbow.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Meaning of the Work?</p><p>It seems that this work is an argument against the established onto-theological view of the world and the rationalism of the Enlightenment. It’s saying that such a view of the world is fundamentally both unable to fully account for the complexities of the world, and also leads us down unproductive and ultimately destructive roads. Melville argues that we should find meaning in the world through concrete, local traditions rather than seeking a single, unified answer in the world at large.</p><blockquote><p>There is, of course, some inherent complexity and impossibility of picking out a single idea from this work, since the most comprehensive one is also the one that undermines the centrality of itself.</p></blockquote></blockquote><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li>Ahab is the <em>Peqoud</em>; the activities and states of the <em>Pequod</em> mirrors that of Ahab.</li><li>Ahab is a slave to his passions; every is a slave to fate/the natural world in some sense</li><li>Ahab is like ambergris?</li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering has metaphysical meaning\">Suffering has metaphysical meaning</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Ideas must be challenged so that they maintain their vitality\">Ideas must be challenged so that they maintain their vitality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry\">Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it\">To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it</a></li></ul><h4>Characters</h4><p><strong>Ishmael</strong></p><ul><li>Impelled by a sense of disillusionment with the world, and thus sought adventure and some meaning in life through the treacherous lifestyle of a whaleman.</li><li>It seems like his character is almost used as a negative foil against which the meaninglessness of the cosmos is portrayed. There are many chapters where Ishmael tries in vain to categorize and rationalize the world, or attach some grand universal meaning to specific aspects of the whale or the business of whaling (e.g., the cetology chapters, the chapter on the glory of the profession of whaling, etc.). However, this is ultimately just used as a device to show that such attempts are in vain.</li><li>I feel like there is an inherent tension in the narrative, however. <em>The very fact that Ishmael survives the journey, and then pens a book about it</em> seems to me, in some sense at least, an knowledge of the existence of a force that arranges the world in a way that Ishmael is trying to deny.<ul><li>It seems that this is either: one, intentional, or two, <strong>a fundamental flaw with any intellectual work that seeks to prove non-existence of some overarching meaning/ordering principle in the world. They are, in a way, self-undermining.</strong></li><li>Ishmael seems to survive because of his refusal to search for a single meaning in the world.</li><li>At the same time:</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ishmael?useskin=vector\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ishmael (biblical figure)</a></p><p>It is a [theophoric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theophoric \"Theophoric\") name translated literally as \"God (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_\\(god\\\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">El</a> \"El (god)\")) has hearkened\", suggesting that \"<strong>a child so named was regarded as the fulfillment of a divine promis</strong>e\".</p><blockquote><p>This is perhaps a simpler explanation for the fact that he survives?</p></blockquote></blockquote><p><strong>Ahab</strong></p><ul><li>He is driven mad by the loss of a leg from Moby Dick.</li><li>It seems that he is unable to understand how meaningless the universe truly is, since he seems incapable of understanding that his loss of a leg is something that is just fundamentally without meaning and a cruel twist of “fate.”</li><li>It is precisely this lack of ability to square the seemingly meaningless nature of the world and the immense suffering of the individual that turns Ahab into the wolfish, heartless creature that he becomes.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Moby-Dick</em>, ch. 36</p><p><em>That inscrutable thing is chiefly what I hate</em>; and be the white whale agent, or be the white whale principal, I will wreak that hate upon him.</p></blockquote><ul><li>Ahab is used in the text as the prototypical figure that holds on to onto-theological ideas. He sees the world as being imbued with meaning, and thus, he is unable to come to terms with the fact that such is actually not the case.<ul><li>Again, an esoteric reading of this could be the following: Ahab does not invent a “theodicy” for the loss of his leg, as is the standard Christian practice. Rather, he often times invokes curses against heaven and engages in other paganistic and blasphemous activities. Melville’s critique then makes the greatest sense as claiming that Christianity places humans in a onto-theological mindset, which can be corrupted and both turned against its creator and also drives ourselves insane (?).</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Moby-Dick</em>, p. 532</p><p>“Ego non baptizo te in nomine patris, sed in nomine diaboli! \\[<strong>I do not baptize you in the name of the Father, but in the name of the devil!</strong>]”</p></blockquote><ul><li>Ahab can be contrasted against the captain of the <em>Samuel Enderby</em>, who suffers the same fate but does not become an Ahab-like figure.</li><li>The biblical Ahab was a devil worshipper.<ul><li>Or perhaps <em>Moby-Dick</em> is a tale akin to an anti-Book of Job, where Ahab is the anti-Job.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Question regarding Ahab</p><p><strong>What to make of the fact that Ahab is seemingly aware of his own insanity and irrationality?</strong> As he says himself on page 530: “In no Paradise myself, I am impatient of all misery in others that is not mad.”</p></blockquote><ul><li>Ahab’s death is a phenomenon of <em>higher-order indifference (whiteness)</em>. <ul><li>Indifference merely means the lack of meaning in the world, but a higher-order indifference is manifest through the existence of various meanings in the world (the condition for the possibility of many different meanings in the world all to be true), but the failure for all these meanings to be able to be synthesized into one.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Pip</strong></p><ul><li>Driven made by his seeing of the fundamental fabric of the world after he is thrown out of the whaling boat.<ul><li>This is the reason that Ahab was afraid of Pip “curing him.”</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Moby-Dick</em>, ch. 129</p><p>There is that in thee, poor lad, which I feel too curing to my malady. Like cures like; <strong>and for this hunt, my malady becomes my most desired health.</strong></p></blockquote><p><strong>Queequeg</strong></p><ul><li>Melville’s example for how to live a fulfilling life.</li><li>He lives without the onto-theology that is associated with Christianity, though he is “poisoned” by Christianity in the book, and thus goes down with the ship at the end of the book.<ul><li>What is telling, however, is the fact that his coffin, upon which he carved all of his esoteric symbols, does not go down with the ship. It is the only part of the ship which survives, and which saves Ishmael.</li><li>The sinking of the <em>Pequod</em> is representative of the death of onto-theology.</li></ul></li><li>Queequeg physically embodies his tradition. It is something that he physically lives by. He has a physical idol as a manifestation of his faith.<ul><li>This concreteness of his religion is what allows for him to be so content. He does not need to pursue philosophy or a meaning for the world, because all of the meaning is already physically present with him.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Moby Dick</strong></p><ul><li>The three days of the final chase is representative of the three different, equally valid interpretations of the nature of the whale.<ol><li>The malice of the whale: the whale acts with cunning in order to wreck Ahab’s boat.</li><li>Awkwardness of the whale/randomness: the whale thrashes and wrecks Ahab’s boat.</li><li>Indifference of the whale: Ahab’s harpoon catches around his neck and kills him.</li></ol></li></ul><h4>Note</h4><ul><li>There are many analogies made in the book to the Bible.</li><li>It seems that if the central meaning of the book is to find meaning through local, concrete traditions, then this book itself must be an exception to the rule? <ul><li>Perhaps this point and the previous point is connected?</li></ul></li><li><strong>The Sacred (Ordinary and Extraordinary):</strong> When the singular “mono-god” collapses, it leaves behind two surviving forms of the sacred that can heal human lives:<ol><li><em>The Extraordinary Sacred:</em> Local, fragile, and temporary religious rituals (like Queequeg's worship of his idol, Yojo).</li><li><em>The Ordinary / Everyday Sacred:</em> The deeply fulfilling everyday practices highlighted in the “Squeeze of the Hand” chapter—such as friendship, a good meal, a bed, a wife, and a fireside.</li></ol></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meaning in History\">Meaning in History</a><ul><li>The point that Melville makes about faith in Christianity is covered in a more scientific way by Lowith. In fact, <em>Moby-Dick</em> is a good case study for the degradation of the idea of faith in Christianity. The characters in the book try to concretize the religion (which Melville views as something that is natural to human nature/a fundamental flaw with Christianity), which is ultimately met by failure.</li><li>In this way, <em>Moby-Dick</em> and <em>Meaning in History</em> have, in a very broad sense, the same thesis: that Christianity cannot survive without its spiritual element, and once earthly elements are introduced (the philosophy of history of Christianity is secularized, while preserving the same Christian framework for understanding history), the entire system implodes.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Bible\">Bible</a><ul><li>Many references are found in this work.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>There are many similarities between these two works in the way that they both approach the topic of man vs. The divine.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All the King&#x27;s Men\">All the King's Men</a><ul><li>There are some similarities in the discussion regarding the fate of humans.</li></ul></li></ul>","Money is not a psychological construct, nor is it simply 'debt'":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Yuval Noah Harari claims that money is a psychological construct that purely the result of our imagination, and David Graeber claims that money is simply “debt.” However, both these points of view are incorrect, since money is an actually economic good.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a rebuttal to the ideas that money is a psychological construct, or that it is simply “debt.”</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Money is a commodity that naturally evolved from barter. It has intrinsic value as a unit of value, a store of value, and as a unit of exchange. Things become money because they serve these purposes well.</p><ul><li>At some point in the past, money had to have value, or was tied to value. It is rather ridiculous for people, at some point, to simple assign value to something that is inherently worthless, since that is impractical, especially on a large scale. <ul><li>You cannot simply gather a group of people and say, “Let's pretend this piece of blue paper is worth one cow.” No one would trust it.</li></ul></li><li>It is simply impossible for there to even be debt before the existence of money, since debt must be denominated in money. <ul><li>Additionally, the state did not money in order to pay its soldiers, since if that was the case, there is no reason for the state to choose gold as its means of paying its soldiers. They must have done so since gold already had inherent value as money.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Money is a psychological construct\">Money is a psychological construct</a><ul><li>This is the idea introduced by Harari regrading the nature of money.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fiat money only has value because it was historically backed by commodities\">Fiat money only has value because it was historically backed by commodities</a><ul><li>This is the Austrian explanation for way fiat money currently has value.</li></ul></li></ul>","Olley-Pakes decomposition produces a more accurate representation of market competitiveness compared to other measures":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Olley-Pakes decomposition, which measures whether consumers are switching to more productive firms, offers a clearer view into the efficiency of a market compared to measure such as HHI (which just looks at size).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This offers a methodological improvement to the problem of ensuring efficient and productive markets. This is a methodological improvement because more market concentration doesn’t necessarily imply reduced innovation and stagnating productivity growth for firms. By looking at Olley-Pakes, we can gain a better understanding into if the concentration is due to the higher productivity of one firm compared to another, or something else.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>The difficulty with this method is just that it requires a lot more data. And in order for this method to be effective, data needs to be collected constantly to ensure that things like monopolies temporarily cutting prices in order to remove competitors from the market is not happening.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Monopolies should make more money\">Monopolies should make more money</a><ul><li>If they are more efficient, it would make sense for them to make more money.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry\">A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry</a><ul><li>What if we investigated natural monopolies with this?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Large MES relative to industry demand indicates consolidation; small MES indicates competition\">Large MES relative to industry demand indicates consolidation; small MES indicates competition</a><ul><li>But more competition isn’t necessarily better.</li></ul></li></ul>","Our identity is defined by our nature as agents":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Whereas Parfit deflates both the metaphysics and the normative importance of the conception of the self, Korsgaard argues that, from a practical standpoint, the second cannot be disregarded.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>She offers a counter-perspective to the argument that Parfit puts forth.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Korsgaard performs a partial Moorean shift. She argues that the metaphysics does not matter, and that only the normative implications are relevant.</p><ul><li>In this way, she even accepts the more conservative and deflated version of the metaphysics of the self, that is Parfit's reductivism.</li></ul><p>She argues that the normative claim must be preserve because if it is not, one would not be able to (or have the motivation to) plan for the future, and rational agency would be incoherent. But she replaces this with a normative claim that we ought to only think of ourselves as only one thing (regardless of whether we actually are) since:</p><ul><li>We have only one body, and therefore, we have to collectively act.</li><li>The agent is the source of the actions that they conduct.</li></ul><p>A future person is only \"you\" because you were able to plan for them.</p><ul><li>A radical change in one's identity that does not originate in the agent disrupts one's identity, but this is not the case if one chooses to change themselves.</li></ul><p>Additionally, collective entities can be people.</p><h4>Refutation</h4><p>But Korsgaard's conception of the self has been challenged via empirical evidence.</p><ul><li><strong>Sperry and Gazzaniga split brain research</strong>: they gave an instruction to one of the brain of a split brain subject, and after the action had been performed, and the other hemisphere of the brain is asked why they had did it, the other half of the brain would post hoc come up with a reason for their action.</li></ul><p>The existence of confabulation undermines the assumption of people as rational and future-thinking agents, which means that it undermines the nature of a person defined as such.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Reductivism regarding personal identity rejects the existence of an egoist self\">Reductivism regarding personal identity rejects the existence of an egoist self</a><ul><li>More about Parfit's metaphysics.</li></ul></li></ul>","Power corrupts those who possess it":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Power is something that begins to change the people who wield the power in negative ways.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common theme both in political theory and also in literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>A main theme of <em>All the King’s Men</em> is the idea that power corrupts individuals, most notably Willie Stark, who, although he began as an individual genuinely trying to help those around him while abiding by moral boundaries, those moral boundaries eventually dissipated, leaving him naked with ambition and a taste for “improving the world” by whatever means necessary. In <em>Ran</em>, the sons of the warlord, especially the first son Taro, is corrupted by the power that he holds as a result of his father’s abdication. As a result, he oversteps his bounds and eventually gets himself killed when he attempts to capture his father at the Third Castle.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Society corrupted innately good humans\">Society corrupted innately good humans</a><ul><li>It is in our nature that we are susceptible to such corruption, or is susceptibility to such corruption a result of the evil influence of society?</li></ul></li></ul>","Striving toward something greater dignifies a death that falls short of it":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An aspiration can confer worth on a life even when it is never reached, so that dying in the attempt is not the same as dying without one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This separates the value of a pursuit from its success, which is what allows failure to be meaningful rather than merely unfortunate.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The figure Hemingway uses is the frozen leopard near the summit of Kilimanjaro, which nobody can explain being there. The counter-reading is that the leopard is simply lost, and the story does not settle which.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Struggles during youth are meaningful\">Struggles during youth are meaningful</a><ul><li>A related claim about the value of unsuccessful effort.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority\">Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority</a><ul><li>A stronger version, and one this claim does not require.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art carries meaning that cannot be paraphrased\">Great art carries meaning that cannot be paraphrased</a><ul><li>Why the ambiguity between the two readings is a feature.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Legalist school taught the theory and method of organization and leadership, not jurisprudence":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Legalist school of China was one focused on teaching people the theory and method of organization and how to wield power, not jurisprudence.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common misconception about the Legalists.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>While the Legalists emphasized the importance of adherence to law in administration, they did not specify what specific laws the ruler should implement. Rather, they argued that the laws should be designed to fit the specific circumstances of state, and that competent officials with such capabilities ought to be the ones designing such laws.</p><h4>References</h4>","The military-industrial complex's may cause the government not in act in the interest of the public":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The close ties between the military and the defense industry manufacturers means that the defense industry manufacturers are able to shape policy, perhaps in such as way as to turn against the interest of the public.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The military-industrial complex may have a substantial impact on public policy due to the extensive influence of the defense industry on the government and the close ties between the two.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>There is a fragmented monopsony when it comes to the military-industrial complex, as internal divisions allow for defense firms to lobby and influence procurement, which may create demand beyond pure strategic need.</li><li>It also persists due to rent-seeking and regional job incentives, which makes then economically sticky.</li><li>There are long-term contracts, which create a lock-in effect that make it difficult for the government to switch suppliers.</li><li>There is a revolving door, as former military officials and policy makers often join defense firms as consultants or executives.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;iron triangle&#x27; of government can lead to regulatory capture\">The 'iron triangle' of government can lead to regulatory capture</a></li></ul>","The minorities in a nation tend to focus on economic advancement":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The minorities in a nation, due to limited access to political influence, are “driven with peculiar force into economic activity.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This helps establish the reason for the Protestants to focus on economic activity, thus finally leading to the development of capitalism.</p><h4>Connection with the Protestant Work Ethic</h4><p>Since the Protestants were the minorities, they focused their efforts mainly on economic advancement. This lead to their development of capitalism.</p><h4>Merchants in China</h4><p>An an interesting parallel of this idea is that merchants were the lowest social class of people in Imperial China. Compared to the peasants, which made up around 90% of the population, they were definitely the minority, who just so happened to also work in a very lucrative profession.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Merchants were the lowest social class in Imperial China\">Merchants were the lowest social class in Imperial China</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a><ul><li>Perhaps minorities are less susceptible to “traditionalism” since the only way that they are able to achieve influence in a nation where they are the minority is through economic advancement.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism\">The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism</a><ul><li>Baptists and Quakers are minorities.</li></ul></li></ul>","The philosophy of history is essentially an elaborate theodicy for all of human existence":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The philosophy of history is essentially the project of understanding the reason for the existence of evil in  world history, and reconciling it with the existence of God.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Hegel’s view of the philosophy of history.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The mechanism that Hegel introduces for this theodicy is that of the cunning of reason.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems however, that this argument is fundamentally unfalsifiable, and the argument proves too much. There is no occurrence that cannot be justified with the cunning of reason. Additionally, the existence of the cunning of reason basically supplies what faith was supposed to do without. It has realized all of the reason behind faith, and thus, there is no use for faith.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Chapter 3, <em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 58</p><p>More decisive than the material limitations of Hegel's vision is the inherent weakness of his principle that the Christian religion is realized by reason in the history of the secular world <strong>as if the Christian faith could ever be \"realized\" at all and yet remain a faith in things unseen!</strong></p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Although humans have free will, they ultimately serve as instruments that express divine will\">Although humans have free will, they ultimately serve as instruments that express divine will</a><ul><li>This is the argument for the cunning of reason.</li></ul></li></ul>","The virtue of art is concerned with making, where the end goal is the product, not the action itself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The virtue of art (<em>techne</em>) is a productive state that is “truly reasoned.” it focuses on the production, i.e., the end goal, rather than the action, i.e., the process by which one reaches the end goal. It deals with the variable, not the necessary/eternal. It involves reason, not just instinct.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The virtue that allows people to create things that don’t necessarily have to exist.</p><h4>References</h4>","We have power over nothing except our will":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that the only thing we can control is ourselves. This usually implies that we should not be distressed by the things that are out of our control.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant stoic idea that has influenced not just followers of the Stoics, but also many other philosophers.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One has to confront their problems head on\">One has to confront their problems head on</a></li></ul>","Windows 11 in 2025":"<p>In the 18 years following the publication of <em>Bad Samaritans</em>, many of economist Ha-Joon Chang’s hypotheses have been put to the test, including his views on patent and intellectual property rights (IPRs) regulation. I will seek to elucidate the veracity of Chang’s claims, made in chapter 6 of his seminal work, <em>Bad Samaritans</em>, “Windows 98 in 1997.” Since 2007, evidence has largely vindicated Chang: patents can spur R&amp;D in high–fixed-cost sectors, but rigid IPR regulation via diluted patent content and interlocking patents raises entry barriers, slows diffusion, and entrenches incumbents.</p><p>In the past 18 years, one thing has remained constant: patents and IPR still exist. And with them remains the fundamental justification for their existence, namely that IPRs incentivize innovation.</p><p>Now, why would IPRs incentivize innovation? It seems that the very nature of IPRs (especially their exclusive, monopolistic nature) would <em>prohibit</em>, not encourage, innovation. To understand this, we must understand the concept of a “natural monopoly.” <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry\">A natural monopoly arises when the fixed costs are high in an industry, but the marginal costs are low. This means that once production, which may be initially expensive, starts, the firm can quickly achieve an economy of scale.</a> The key component to consider is the high initial costs of production, which necessitate that few are able to meet the high capital requirement. Those who are able to afford such capital requirements thereby take on immense risk, which demands that the reward be adequately commensurate. Here is where patents and IPRs come in. As monopolies provide their holders with disproportionate surplus, they can be utilized as a form of reward for the risk-takers, without whom innovations in science and technology would not have occurred. If there had been no legal protection of the result of such a high capital investment in the form of protections for IPRs, competitors could create imitations, thereby bringing the price—and thus profit—down for the original capital owner.</p><p>Herein lies the nucleus of Chang’s contention: the government controls the length of a patent and, thereby, the size of the carrot which leads people to take substantial risks.</p><p>But how big, <em>exactly</em>, ought the carrot be?</p><p>Chang argued that the world’s governments have provided too generous a benefit for capital owners, which, paradoxically, leads to the originally unparadoxical conclusion: that such patents and IPRs create a barrier to innovation and benefit only incumbents in the market. But is this the case? Let us examine his argument:</p><p>Returning to the discussion regarding the fundamental justification for patents and natural monopolies, Chang puts forth a challenge, arguing that <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas\">material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas</a>. He points to the amount of research conducted by non-profit and government organizations, which seems to be testament to the idea that research need not be conducted by profit-seeking private actors.[@changBadSamaritansMyth2008, p. 124-125] However, there are a few inaccuracies in such an argument. First, from 1996–2016, 67 to 97 percent of drug development was conducted by the private sector.[@chakravarthyPublicPrivateSectorContributions2016, p. 2] This seems to undermine the claim that non-material incentives play a substantial role in funding investment in new research. Yes, perhaps some research into pharmaceuticals was conducted via government funding or through private charities, but the contribution of funding through such sources pales in comparison with private, for-profit funding. In addition, much of the government research focuses on “basic science,” i.e., research that, although crucial, does not directly contribute to the development of pharmaceuticals. Rather, it is primarily private institutions that carry out the expensive, risky, and highly specialized process of creating actual medicines.[@chakravarthyPublicPrivateSectorContributions2016, p. 5, 10]</p><p>Chang also makes the claim that patents are not needed in most industries, as often new inventions are sufficiently difficult to imitate to provide the innovator with a substantive first-mover advantage.[@changBadSamaritansMyth2008, p.125-126] Regarding this, Chang seems to be mistaken: the pharmaceutical industry is an example of an industry where it is extremely difficult to make imitations of competitors’ products.[@kirchhoffBiosimilarsKeyRegulatory2017] Yet, around 65 percent of commercial inventions in the pharmaceutical industry would not have existed had patents not been granted.[@cockburnImportancePatentsInnovation2015, p.740] That being said, Chang’s claim is indeed applicable in some industries; for example, the fashion industry has achieved what some term a “low-IP equilibrium,” where rampant copying actually drives innovation.[@raustialaPiracyParadoxInnovation2006, p. 1732-1734] In these industries, the existence of patents would only impede progress.</p><p>Industries that require patents and IPRs face a further problem, however: new patents are increasingly trivial, even potentially malicious, which Chang attributes to <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs\">inventors’ incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs</a>. First, he points out, there is theft of traditional knowledge from developing nations; knowledge that, despite being widely known in its area of origin, has been patented by other countries.[@changBadSamaritansMyth2008, p.137] Second, he identifies the phenomenon of “<a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">interlocking patents</a>”: patents have tended to be increasingly fragmented and minute, meaning that any new major innovation would have to navigate a landscape of dozens, perhaps hundreds, of other relevant patents [@changBadSamaritansMyth2008, p.139-140]. Regarding his former concern, systems instituted since the publication of his work have now placed much stricter limits on the ability of nations to patent traditional knowledge. For example, the 2025 WIPO Treaty on Intellectual Property, Genetic Resources and Associated Traditional Knowledge (GRATK) will “enhance the efficacy, transparency and quality of the patent system with regard to genetic resources and traditional knowledge…, and prevent patents from being granted erroneously for inventions that are not novel…”[@alvesWIPOsNewTreaty2024] In addition, many countries have implemented laws or compiled traditional knowledge databases to further prevent theft of traditional knowledge.[@gutierrezHowLatinAmerica2020; @canadianintellectualpropertyofficePatentsBasedGenetic2025] Regarding the latter concern, while it is very much extant, there are no “easy” potential solutions to implement. “Patent pools,” where all members of an industry cross-license all relevant patents to one another, are difficult to coordinate, especially without government intervention. Yet, excessive government intervention in promoting patent pools could, once more, stifle innovation. The problem of interlocking patents identified by Chang is a complex one and will require nuanced regulation and institutional reform in order to mitigate it, being beyond the scope of this short essay.</p><p>Ultimately, Chang claims that a strict and overregulated patent and IPR regime (in the manners previously described) hurts developing countries.[@changBadSamaritansMyth2008, p.141] The high cost of running a patent office, especially when having to comply with variegated international regulations, acts as <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">a drag on the economies of developing, less technologically advanced nations</a>. International regulations represent a misallocation of resources that prevent less affluent countries from investing in crucial aspects of government functions, such as education, health care, or law enforcement. Moreover, they prevent developing nations from being able to acquire new knowledge in the long term, thereby deterring these nations from further economic development. Recent evidence bears this out. Implementing international IPR regulation has indeed imposed measurable administrative and fiscal burdens on low-income states in the form of fixed outlays to stand up offices, recurrent enforcement costs, and royalty payments.[@maskusIntellectualPropertyChallenges2001, p. 466-470] Moreover, comparative reviews find that without strong local absorptive capacity, regulation chiefly advantages incumbents, raising prices and constraining economic growth rather than benefiting developing nations as a whole.[@hassanIntellectualPropertyDeveloping, p.27-30]</p><p>Chang’s conclusion that IPR should be designed to help developing countries be more productive and attain new technology is a cogent one. Thus, while the “carrot” of monopoly remains defended as necessary for innovation, and some empirical data remain contrary to Chang’s claims, the overall evidence leans in favor of Chang’s conclusion: as global inequality increases, it is up to our international organizations to recognize the faults in their systems, rectify them, and promote a more equitable and inclusive global community.</p><h4>Bibliography</h4><p>Alves, Ayla Do Vale. WIPO’s New Treaty on Intellectual Property, Genetic Resources and Traditional Knowledge – A Turning Point for Indigenous Heritage? May 24, 2024. https://esil-sedi.eu/esil-reflection-wipos-new-trea ty-on-intellectual-property-genetic-resources-and-traditional-knowledge-aturning-point-for-indigenous-heritage/.</p><p>Canadian Intellectual Property Office. “Patents based on genetic resources and associated traditional knowledge.” Innovation, Science and Economic Development Canada, June 16, 2025. https://ised-isde.canada.ca/site/canadianintellectual-property-office/en/patents-based-genetic-resources-andassociated-traditional-knowledge.</p><p>Chakravarthy, Ranjana, Kristina Cotter, Joseph DiMasi, Christopher-Paul Milne, and Nils Wendel. “Public- and Private-Sector Contributions to the Research and Development of the Most Transformational Drugs in the Past 25 Years: From Theory to Therapy.” Therapeutic Innovation &amp; Regulatory Science 50, no. 6 (November 2016): 759–68. https://doi.org/10.1177/2168479016648730.</p><p>Chang, Ha-Joon. Bad samaritans: the myth of free trade and the secret history of capitalism. New York: Bloomsbury, 2008.</p><p>Cockburn, Iain, and Genia Long. “The Importance of Patents to Innovation: Updated Cross-Industry Comparisons with Biopharmaceuticals.” Expert Opinion on Therapeutic Patents 25, no. 7 (July 2015): 739–42. https: //doi.org/10.1517/13543776.2015.1040762.</p><p>Gutiérrez, Nicolás. “How Latin America Countries Protect Their Traditional Knowledge Through IP - European Commission.” IP Helpdesk, January 16, 2020. https://intellectual-property-helpdesk.ec.europa.eu/news-events /news/how-latin-america-countries-protect-their-traditional-knowledgethrough-ip-2020-01-16\\_en.</p><p>Hassan, Emmanuel, Ohid Yaqub, and Stephanie Diepeveen. Intellectual Property and Developing Countries: A Review of the Literature. n.d.</p><p>Kirchhoff, Carol F., Xiao-Zhuo Michelle Wang, Hugh D. Conlon, Scott Anderson, Anne M. Ryan, and Arindam Bose. “Biosimilars: Key Regulatory Considerations and Similarity Assessment Tools.” Biotechnology and Bioengineering 114, no. 12 (2017): 2696–705. https://doi.org/10.1002/bit.26438.</p><p>Maskus, Keith E. “Intellectual Property Challenges for Developing Countries: An Economic Perspective.” University of Illinois Law Review, April 16, 2001. https://www.researchgate.net/publication/294496476\\_Intellectual\\_proper ty\\_challenges\\_for\\_developing\\_countries\\_An\\_economic\\_perspective.</p><p>Raustiala, Kal, and Christopher Sprigman. “The Piracy Paradox: Innovation and Intellectual Property in Fashion Design - Virginia Law Review.” Virginia Law Review 92, no. 8 (December 2006). https://virginialawreview.org/arti cles/piracy-paradox-innovation-and-intellectual-property-fashion-design/.</p>"});
